cartridge "PINHEAD/JAMES MERLO/1990"

' PINHEAD
' A ColecoVision game based on "Pinhead", COMPUTE!'s Gazette February 1990
' (Commodore 64 machine language, 4,028 bytes at $0801 started by SYS 2064,
' a packed program that unpacks itself to $5000; no author named on the
' disk). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in
' Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' machine code, orig/code5000.dis, and watched in jsc64), the game is
' built for the ColecoVision: a night circus ring under bunting, bunches
' of balloons at both sides, a little pin-headed acrobat, a trampoline,
' smooth sprites and sounds.
'
' The game: move the trampoline left and right and press the button to
' launch Pinhead. Keep him bouncing: where he lands on the trampoline sets
' how high he flies (the middle sends him highest) and which way he
' drifts. Balloons float across the tent; pop them with Pinhead. A miss
' costs a life; five misses (LIVES 4 to 0, then one more) end the game.
'
' From the original: the trampoline (two sprites) moves 3 pixels a frame,
' 6 with the button held; Pinhead sits on it until the button launches
' him on the middle arc; eight arcs, chosen by the landing offset from the
' trampoline's middle (the centre highest, the ends lowest); landing left
' of the middle sends him left, right of it right, his drift speed random
' (0..1, 0..3 from 300 points, 0..7 from 1500); he bounces off the sides;
' missing the trampoline drops him to the floor, a dizzy pause, a life
' lost; up to six balloons, a new one every 100 frames (90 from 100
' points, 80 from 300, 70 from 500, 60 from 900, 50 from 1200, 35 from
' 1500, 25 from 2000) if a slot is free, each from the left or right edge
' at a random height, speed (1, 1..3 from 500 points, 1..7 from 2000),
' colour and one of 16 sizes; a pop scores 10 x the balloon's speed plus
' 1-9 for its size (the smallest most), turns Pinhead round and gives him
' a new drift speed (0..balloon speed mask); a life every 2000 points (at
' most 9); LIVES starts at 4.
'
' Liberties: coordinates scaled to the TV-safe field (columns 2-29, rows
' 1-22: 0.74 vertically, about 0.77 across) and speeds to 60 frames a
' second (the C64 ran at 50): the trampoline moves 2 pixels a frame, 4
' with the button; drift and balloon speeds are kept in quarter pixels
' (0, 3, 5, 8, 10, 13, 15, 18 per original step); the eight flight tables
' are replaced by gravity (38/256 pixel per frame per frame) and a launch
' speed per arc reaching the scaled apex of each table; box collisions
' instead of the VIC's pixel collisions; balloons appear and leave at the
' field's edges (the C64's came out of the side borders); balloon colours
' from eight bright ones that show on the dark tent; the original sent
' Pinhead straight up (no drift) at the launch and from a dead-centre
' landing, so a player who never moved kept him bouncing for ever: here
' the launch and every landing give a drift of at least one step; the
' paddle mode (F1/F3) is left out (joystick only); a title, credits and a
' GAME OVER page (the C64 waited for the button on the play field).
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2): the stick moves the trampoline, a button
' launches Pinhead (held: the trampoline moves faster). On the title, up
' and down choose, a button chooses.

